WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.460
Track every ticket, stay honest, and measure your work.

2
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:07.850
This is the video for module 3 of Online Customer Support.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:18.010
A ticket is simply a record of one customer request, from the moment it arrives until it is closed. A free Google Sheet can do this job.

4
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:24.260
Written policies and FAQs mean every customer gets the same fair answer.

5
00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:29.450
And metrics turn I work hard into evidence an employer can see.

6
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.460
Give each ticket a number, and write the real time it was received.

7
00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:40.040
Note the customer's first name and the channel, so you can find the chat again.

8
00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:49.120
Choose a category, like delivery or wrong item, and a priority. Use dropdown lists so everyone types the same words.

9
00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:56.980
Record your first reply time and the status: New, Waiting for customer, Waiting for us, or Resolved.

10
00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:05.720
Finally, the resolved time and a short note of what happened. Every evening, filter for Waiting for us and set reminders.

11
00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:12.850
Protect the sheet. Share it only with colleagues who need it, and never store bank details or ID numbers in it.

12
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:30.660
High priority means money taken but no goods, any safety issue, or a customer who has already chased twice. For example, Ruth logs a customer who paid 23,000 naira two days ago with no delivery. She chases dispatch at once.

13
00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:38.650
Normal priority covers a wrong item, a late delivery within a reasonable time, and most complaints.

14
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:46.170
Low priority covers general questions and feedback. Answer them, but after the urgent cases.

15
00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:54.950
If a customer reports a skin reaction, illness after food, or a shock from a device, treat it as High priority.

16
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.870
Express concern. Never say, nobody else has complained.

17
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.800
Advise her to stop using the product and to see a doctor or pharmacist if she has symptoms.

18
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.970
Ask for the product name, batch number and photos.

19
00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:20.410
Escalate to the owner immediately. Food, drugs and cosmetics fall under NAFDAC, and the owner may need to act on the batch.

20
00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.590
Remember, you give no medical advice and suggest no treatments.

21
00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:35.860
Customers trust a business that keeps its word. So do not promise what you cannot control, do not hide known problems, and never make up a reason.

22
00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:47.580
Say the courier estimates Thursday. Tell waiting customers about a delay before they chase you. And when you do not know, say: I will find out and update you by 2 pm.

23
00:02:48.830 --> 00:03:00.200
First response time is how long customers wait for the first human reply. Ada logs 5 tickets. The waits are 7, 25, 6, 48 and 14 minutes.

24
00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.990
Added together, that is 100 minutes.

25
00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:08.740
Divide by 5 tickets, and her average is 20 minutes.

26
00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:18.930
An average can hide problems. One ticket waited 48 minutes, while Ada was in a meeting. So she plans to check chats between meetings.

27
00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:23.720
Only a real person's reply counts as a first response.

28
00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:34.570
Resolution rate is resolved tickets divided by tickets received, times 100. Ada resolved 72 of 80, so 90%.

29
00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:43.820
First contact resolution is the share solved in the first reply. 45 of 72 gives 62.5%.

30
00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:57.940
CSAT, customer satisfaction, is happy ratings divided by all responses. 34 of 40 gave 4 or 5, so 85%. Always say how many customers answered.

31
00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:03.530
And never close tickets too quickly just to raise a number. That lowers satisfaction.

32
00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:13.870
To remember. Log every request as a ticket. Money and safety come first. For any harm, advise stopping use and escalate at once.

33
00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:21.070
Build your FAQs from your most common categories. And end every half-page weekly report with one action.

34
00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:36.270
Your turn. Build your tracker and log at least 10 tickets. Write a one-page policy, three FAQ answers and three escalation levels. Then calculate your metrics and write a half-page report with one action.

35
00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.560
Continue with the lessons below.
